 "chunks": [
  {
   "text": "Start with the primes: two, three, five, seven, eleven,",
   "start": 0.725,
   "end": 4.438
  },
  {
   "text": "whole numbers above one with no divisors but one and themselves.",
   "start": 4.438,
   "end": 8.438
  },
  {
   "text": "Up close, they look random.",
   "start": 8.438,
   "end": 10.425
  },
  {
   "text": "But count them.",
   "start": 10.425,
   "end": 11.612
  },
  {
   "text": "π(x) is the number of primes up to x.",
   "start": 11.612,
   "end": 13.737
  },
  {
   "text": "Its graph is a staircase, and from far away the staircase looks astonishingly",
   "start": 13.737,
   "end": 18.162
  },
  {
   "text": "smooth.",
   "start": 18.162,
   "end": 19.075
  },
  {
   "text": "As a teenager, Gauss guessed that near x,",
   "start": 19.975,
   "end": 22.413
  },
  {
   "text": "roughly one number in ln x is prime.",
   "start": 22.413,
   "end": 25.963
  },
  {
   "text": "Adding up those odds gives the logarithmic integral,",
   "start": 25.963,
   "end": 29.013
  },
  {
   "text": "li(x).",
   "start": 29.013,
   "end": 29.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Up to a million there are 78,498 primes,",
   "start": 29.85,
   "end": 34.625
  },
  {
   "text": "and L I is off by only about a hundred and thirty.",
   "start": 34.625,
   "end": 37.838
  },
  {
   "text": "The real question is: how big can that error get?",
   "start": 37.838,
   "end": 41.275
  }
 ],
